    "markScheme": "Levels (6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1-2): a simple comment about a feature, with little or no supporting information.\nLevel 2 (3-4): features are identified and some information is added. Maximum 3 marks if only ONE feature is covered.\nLevel 3 (5-6): two features are described clearly with good, accurate knowledge of the period.\nExaminer tip: make it obvious you are giving two separate features (e.g. 'One feature was... A second feature was...'). Short, precise answers can get full marks.\n\nIndicative content (other valid points must also be credited):\nApril Theses:\n- A political programme Lenin issued as soon as he returned to Russia in April 1917 (via the 'sealed train' from Switzerland, with German help).\n- Called for no support for the Provisional Government and for a second revolution - 'All power to the Soviets'.\n- Demanded an end to the war, and land for the peasants - summed up in 'Peace, Bread and Land'.\nTreaty of Brest-Litovsk:\n- Signed in March 1918 between Bolshevik Russia and the Central Powers (led by Germany and Austria-Hungary); it took Russia out of the First World War.\n- Russia lost huge areas: Finland, Poland, the Baltic states, Belarus and Ukraine - including much of its population, farmland and industry.\n- Russia had to pay heavy reparations (six billion marks).\nExaminer note: Brest-Litovsk was very well known; strong answers explained Russia's losses and why the Bolsheviks needed to leave the war."
