    "markScheme": "Levels (6 marks: 2 knowledge, 4 explanation):\nLevel 1 (1-2): simple comment on a difference, with general information.\nLevel 2 (3-4): differences are explained with specific information. Maximum 3 marks if only ONE difference is covered.\nLevel 3 (5-6): two differences are explained with explicit comparison, supported by specific information about BOTH dates.\nExaminer tip: start each point with the criterion, e.g. 'One difference was the scale of industry: in 1867... whereas by 1895...', rather than describing each date separately.\n\nIndicative content (other valid points must also be credited):\n- Industry: in 1867 production was mainly small-scale craft and cottage industry; by 1895 Meiji industrialisation had brought factory mass production, especially of textiles such as silk and cotton.\n- Economic system: in 1867 Japan had a feudal, rice-based economy under the daimyo; by 1895 feudalism had ended and a more capitalist economy had grown, with banks (e.g. the Bank of Japan, 1882) financing growth.\n- Infrastructure and trade: railways, telegraph, ports and growing foreign trade by 1895, compared with very limited links in 1867.\n- Education/workforce: more educated workers after compulsory schooling (1872)."
