    "markScheme": "Levels (16 marks: 7 knowledge, 9 analysis and judgement):\nLevel 1 (1-4): simple or general answer with little development; limited knowledge; judgement missing or just asserted.\nLevel 2 (5-8): some explanation, but links to the question are mostly implicit; some accurate knowledge; a judgement is given but not properly justified. Maximum 6 marks if the answer does not go beyond two aspects.\nLevel 3 (9-12): explanation mostly focused on the question with a generally sustained line of argument; good, accurate knowledge; judgement has some justification but the criteria are partly implicit. Maximum 10 marks if the answer does not go beyond two aspects.\nLevel 4 (13-16): analytical explanation consistently focused on the question, coherent and logically structured; wide-ranging, precisely chosen knowledge; clear criteria are used to reach a justified overall judgement. Must go beyond two aspects (use at least one point of the student's own beyond the two prompts).\nThis question is about CHANGE: explain the extent of change, not a narrative of events.\nExaminer tip: lots of knowledge is not enough. Explain how each factor links to the question, weigh it against the stated factor, and give a judgement based on clear criteria (e.g. which had the longest-lasting impact, which made the others happen).\n\nIndicative content (other valid points must also be credited):\nChange:\n- Opening to the West led to admiration of Western ideas; the West was no longer seen as 'barbarian', and some wanted to copy more liberal ideas.\n- Meiji-era Westernisation of dress, music, art and newspapers - very different from Tokugawa society.\n- The end of feudalism and the samurai class: people could choose their jobs and travel freely.\n- Taisho period: more money, leisure and education; city life and Western influences weakened the authority of the extended family.\n- Industrialisation brought new values of profit, efficiency, materialism and individualism.\nLimited change:\n- Meiji Japan was still repressive: no free press or academic freedom; the emperor was sacred.\n- Women gained no political voice and little legal standing under the Meiji.\n- Under Taisho, the middle classes gained most; workers had little status, and working-class protests (e.g. the 1918 rice riots) were put down."
